<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quba</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guba, Kuba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quba is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> on the slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a>, near the Caspian coast. In the 18th century it was the seat of the Quba Khanate, whose ruler Fatali Khan united much of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> occupied it in 1806. Across the river lies Krasnaya Sloboda, one of the largest Mountain Jewish settlements in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quba.jpg" alt="Quba" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gulustan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quba_385.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Quba is the administrative centre of the Quba District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. It lies on the north-eastern slopes of the Shahdag mountain, at an altitude of 600 metres, on the right bank of the Kudyal River, 168 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. The city grew from a riverside village called Gudial. The Quba region has a humid subtropical climate. The great majority of its people are Azerbaijanis, with Tats and Lezgians as minorities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>European geographers and ancient Arabic and Albanian sources mention the place. A castle built by the ruler Anushiravan in the 11th century was called Bade-Firuz Qubat. Arabic sources of the 12th century name it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, a geographical dictionary of the 13th century has Kubba, and sources of the 16th century call it Dome.</p><p>In the mid-18th century Hussain Ali moved his residence from Khudat and became khan of Quba, raising fortress walls around the city. He tried to create a state separate from the other Caucasian khanates. The Quba Khanate grew stronger under his son Fatali Khan, who reigned from 1758 to 1789. Like the other Transcaucasian khanates it was occupied by Czarist Russia in the early 19th century and formally annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> under the agreement of 1813. Quba was included in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> province in 1840 and in 1860 became part of the Kuba Uyezd of the Baku Governorate. Alexandre Dumas, the orientalist Berezin, the writer Bestuzhev-Marlinsky and the Norwegian Thor Heyerdahl are said to have visited it.</p><p>Quba has long been a centre of carpet weaving: the carpet Golu Chichi was woven there in 1712 and has been shown at the Metropolitan Museum of Art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. In 2007 a mass grave was found of victims killed in 1918 during the events known as the March Days, and a memorial was opened on the site in 2013.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Quba's suburb of Qırmızı Qəsəbə, formerly Krasnaya Sloboda, meaning Red Town, is home to the largest community of Mountain Jews in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, one of the largest Jewish populations in the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. The city's red-brick Juma Mosque was erected in 1802, an octagonal building with a dome 16 m across. The Sakina-Khanum Mosque dates from 1854, and the Chuhur hamam is a bathhouse with a brick beehive dome, used until 1985. The bridge over the Kudyalchay, built in 1894 with 14 brick spans and a length of 275 m, is the only one of seven bridges once in the district to survive. Local cuisine reflects its multicultural history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rivalry with neighbouring Kaitag and <b>Quba</b> weakened it, and in 1765 Fatali Khan of the Quba Khanate seized the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/derbent-khanate">Derbent Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The khanate was drawn into the conflicts of its neighbours, notably the <b>Quba</b> khanate, the Talysh Khanate and the Karabakh Khanate, and was briefly occupied by Fath Ali Khan of Quba.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-ardabil">Khanate of Ardabil</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kuba</b> oral history was studied in detail by the historian Jan Vansina.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuba-kingdom">Kuba Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The khanate grew out of the <b>Quba</b> district on the Caspian slope of the Caucasus.</blockquote><a href="/state/quba-khanate">Quba Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Tabasaran was a vassal of the Safavid Empire for much of this period and was drawn into the struggles between the khans of Derbent and <b>Quba</b>, the Ottomans and Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/tabasaran-maysumate">Tabasaran Maysumate</a></li></ul></section>
